==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|tepoy}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtiː.pɔɪ/|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|hi|तिपाई|tr=-}} / {{bor|en|ur|تپائی|tr=tipāi|t=teapoy}}, which derives from a merger of {{der|en|sa|त्रिपाद|t=tripod}} and {{der|en|fa-cls|سه پای|tr=sih-pāy|t=tripod}}. Corresponding ultimately to {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} {{com|ine-pro|*tréyes|*pṓds|t1=three|t2=foot}}. Spelling influenced by pseudoetymological association with {{m|en|tea}}.
 
===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Originally, a [[three-legged]] [[decorative]] [[stand]] or [[table]]; sometimes also having a [[tea chest]] for holding a [[tea service]]. {{defdate|from 1820s}}
#* '''2019''', Nancy E. Davis, ''[https://books.google.co.in/books?id=IdGaDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA107&dq=teapoy&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi82OmM5d7kAhVG8HMBHSKGAvAQ6AEIOTAD#v=onepage&q=teapoy&f=false The Chinese Lady: Afong Moy in Early America]'':
#*: The '''teapoy''', derived from the Hindi/Persian phrase denoting a three-footed table, supported a tea set or tea-related objects. Not all '''teapoys''' were tripod-like tables or sold singly. The Carneses imported lacquer '''teapoys''' in sets. These sets could be easily stacked in a corner of the drawing room and brought out at teatime to hold a teacup, a set, or a caddy. The Carneses purchased lacquered '''teapoys''' sets for four dollars in China and probably sold them for twice that amount in America.

